WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.800
Dynamic allocation creates storage whose lifetime is controlled by allocation and deallocation calls.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:11.540
malloc allocates uninitialized storage, while calloc initializes allocated storage to zero bytes.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.440
Neither call removes the need to construct valid object state before the program reads from that storage.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.100
Every allocation needs a clear owner and a matching release strategy.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:27.720
The owner might be the caller, the callee, a container object, or a cleanup routine, but the contract should be visible.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:34.020
Allocation failure must also be handled safely because a null result or partial initialization can change the cleanup path.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.000
realloc deserves special attention because it can move storage.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.340
If it succeeds, old pointers and aliases may no longer designate the live storage.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.080
If it fails, the original allocation remains valid.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:55.300
Assigning realloc directly to the original pointer can lose the only reference to the original allocation if the call fails, so review patterns carefully.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.960
Allocation size calculations must be checked before allocation.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:06.380
Multiplication, addition, and element-size conversions can overflow size_t and produce a smaller allocation than the program expects.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.120
Defensive code calculates sizes in a way that makes overflow checks explicit before memory is requested.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:18.260
After free, the object's lifetime has ended, and pointers that designate it must not be used.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.320
Setting one pointer to null may help local cleanup clarity, but it does not update every alias.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:31.180
Cleanup code should handle partial initialization and multiple failure paths without leaks or double-release behavior.
